CAT = "uksiaVenue"; SEG = "VEN"
# Unit 2, LO2, LO5, LO6 and LO7: searching, licensing law (England and Wales), queues and capacity, equipment.
Q = [
("What gives a door supervisor the right to search a customer?",
 "The customer's consent", ["The SIA licence itself", "The Licensing Act 2003", "A police warrant"],
 "Door supervisors have no legal power to search. They search only with the person's permission, usually as a condition of entry under the admissions policy."),
("Searching every fourth person in the queue is which type of search?",
 "Random", ["General", "Specific", "Premises"],
 "A random search picks people by a set pattern, such as every fourth person. A general search covers everyone; a specific search targets someone for a particular reason."),
("Searching a particular customer because staff saw them hide something is which type of search?",
 "Specific", ["Random", "General", "Premises"],
 "A specific search is of a particular person for a particular reason. A general search is of everyone entering."),
("A customer refuses to be searched where searching is a condition of entry. What should you do?",
 "Refuse entry and record it", ["Search them anyway", "Let them in but watch them", "Call the police to search them"],
 "Politely explain that searching is a condition of entry. If they still refuse, deny entry, follow venue policy and record it in the search register."),
("Where possible, where should searches be carried out?",
 "In view of CCTV with a witness", ["In a private room, alone", "Out of sight of any cameras", "Behind the bar, out of the way"],
 "Use a dedicated search area, search with a witness or in view of CCTV and use signs to say searching takes place. This protects both you and the customer."),
("A customer does not want to be touched during a search. What can you offer?",
 "A self-search while you watch", ["A search by the manager instead", "Entry with no search at all", "A search once they are inside"],
 "Self-search techniques let the customer empty pockets and pat down their own clothing while you observe. It also reduces infection risk."),
("When searching a child or young person, what should you do?",
 "Have a responsible adult present", ["Search them away from any adult", "Do it quickly without explaining", "Ask them to remove their clothes"],
 "Search in the presence of a parent, guardian or other responsible adult, explain what is happening, get their consent, use someone of the same sex and never ask them to remove more than outer garments."),
("Which PPE helps protect against needle injuries when searching?",
 "Needle-resistant gloves", ["Thin latex gloves", "A high-visibility vest", "Ear defenders"],
 "Needle and slash resistant gloves protect against sharps. Thin latex gloves help against infection but not against a needle."),
("You find an item that breaks venue rules but is legal, such as a large umbrella. What might you do?",
 "Hold it and return it on exit", ["Keep it permanently", "Call the police at once", "Arrest the customer"],
 "For a legal item that breaks entry policy, follow venue policy. That may mean holding it until the customer leaves. Illegal items are a matter for seizure and the police."),
("Why is the venue searched before it opens?",
 "To find hazards and hidden items", ["To count the stock behind the bar", "To check staff licences", "To test the fire alarm"],
 "A pre-entry premises search checks the venue is safe to open, looking for hazards, drugs, weapons and suspicious packages. A closing check makes sure nobody is left inside."),
("How many licensing objectives are there under the Licensing Act 2003 in England and Wales?",
 "Four", ["Three", "Five", "Six"],
 "There are four: prevention of crime and disorder, public safety, prevention of public nuisance and protection of children from harm. Scotland's law has a fifth, on public health."),
("Which of these is a licensing objective under the Licensing Act 2003 in England and Wales?",
 "Prevention of public nuisance", ["Protection of public health", "Promotion of late-night trade", "Reduction of drink prices"],
 "Prevention of public nuisance is one of the four objectives. Protecting and improving public health is an objective only under Scottish licensing law."),
("Who has day-to-day responsibility for running premises licensed to sell alcohol?",
 "The designated premises supervisor", ["The head door supervisor", "The police force's licensing officer", "The local council"],
 "The designated premises supervisor (DPS) has day-to-day responsibility, is named in the premises licence and is the point of contact for the police and council."),
("How many designated premises supervisors can be named for one premises?",
 "One", ["Two", "Three", "As many as needed"],
 "A premises licence that allows alcohol sales names only one designated premises supervisor at a time."),
("Which body grants premises licences in England and Wales?",
 "The local licensing authority", ["The Security Industry Authority", "The local police force", "The Home Office"],
 "Premises and personal licences are granted by the licensing authority, which is the local council. The police can object and can apply for a review or closure."),
("A customer refuses to leave licensed premises after being properly asked. What do they become?",
 "A trespasser", ["A detained person", "A person under arrest", "An exempt guest"],
 "Anyone who refuses to leave when asked becomes a trespasser and can be removed using only such force as is reasonable and necessary."),
("Under the Licensing Act 2003, it is an offence to knowingly sell alcohol to someone who is:",
 "Drunk", ["Over 70", "A tourist", "Not a member"],
 "Selling or attempting to sell alcohol to a person who is drunk is an offence. So is knowingly allowing disorderly conduct on licensed premises."),
("Under section 143 of the Licensing Act 2003, a drunk person who refuses to leave when asked:",
 "Commits an offence", ["Must be arrested by staff", "Can be fined by the venue", "Breaks civil law only"],
 "Section 143 makes it an offence for a drunk or disorderly person to fail, without reasonable excuse, to leave licensed premises when asked. A constable must help expel them if requested."),
("What is the minimum age to buy alcohol in licensed premises in England and Wales?",
 "18", ["16", "17", "21"],
 "Selling alcohol to anyone under 18 is an offence. Venues can face penalties and test purchasing takes place."),
("A 17-year-old is having a table meal with an adult who buys their drink. What may they drink in England and Wales?",
 "Beer, wine or cider", ["Any alcoholic drink", "Spirits mixed with soft drinks", "No alcohol at all"],
 "A 16 or 17-year-old may drink beer, wine or cider with a table meal if an adult buys it and they are accompanied by an adult. Spirits are never allowed."),
("Buying alcohol for someone under 18 in a bar, with no table meal, is:",
 "An offence", ["Allowed for 17-year-olds", "Legal if a parent buys it", "A civil matter only"],
 "Buying or attempting to buy alcohol on behalf of a person under 18 is an offence, whoever buys it. The only exception is beer, wine or cider with a table meal for 16 and 17-year-olds."),
("Which is an acceptable proof of age under most venue policies?",
 "A photocard driving licence", ["A bank card with a name", "A copy of a birth certificate", "A photo of a passport"],
 "Accepted ID usually means a passport, photocard driving licence or proof-of-age card with a hologram. Follow your venue's policy."),
("Under a Challenge 25 policy, who should be asked for ID?",
 "Anyone who looks under 25", ["Anyone who looks under 18", "Anyone who looks under 21", "Everyone who looks over 25"],
 "Challenge 25 means asking for ID from anyone who appears to be under 25, which gives a safety margin above the legal age of 18."),
("What is one benefit of good queue control?",
 "Less risk of conflict outside", ["More drinks sold outside", "No need to search anyone", "Faster police response times at night"],
 "Good queue control lowers the risk of conflict, lets you assess attitude and sobriety, enforces the admissions policy and keeps customers safe."),
("A venue has reached its capacity. What should the door team do?",
 "Stop entry until people leave", ["Let in regular customers only", "Ask the DJ to slow the music", "Open the fire exits for air"],
 "Halt entry once capacity is reached and count people in and out. This keeps the venue within its licence, fire safety rules and health and safety law."),
("Which equipment is used to count customers in and out?",
 "A clicker counter", ["A search wand", "A breathalyser", "A body-worn camera"],
 "Clickers and other counters track numbers. With several entrances, staff radio each other for updated totals."),
("Why should a dispersal policy be followed at closing time?",
 "To help customers leave safely", ["To move everyone to another bar", "To keep the bar open longer", "To check ID on the way out"],
 "Dispersal procedures help customers leave safely, prevent disorder and nuisance outside, support the police and help the venue meet the licensing objectives."),
("Which is a purpose of a body-worn camera?",
 "Securing evidence of an offence", ["Replacing written incident reports", "Monitoring staff breaks", "Counting customers inside"],
 "Body-worn cameras secure evidence, deter crime, protect staff, curb behaviour on both sides and help identify offenders. You still write a report."),
]
